1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,283
Someone sends you a screenshot: “Where
do I press?” On a call, neither of you

2
00:00:04,333 --> 00:00:08,617
can see what the other means. ShowMe
sends back one clear visual next step.

3
00:00:08,667 --> 00:00:12,950
This example uses made-up billing
details.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,830
I add the screenshot and the goal:
cancel my subscription. The browser

5
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:26,710
detects structured details such as
emails and card numbers. Detection is

6
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:32,590
partial, so I review the picture and
brush out this name myself. Only after

7
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:38,470
I continue does the reviewed, redacted
image go to ShowMe and Meta.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,910
Muse Spark identifies the next
control. SAM can refine the highlight

9
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:52,870
when its bounding box agrees with
Spark’s and contains Spark’s point.

10
00:00:52,920 --> 00:00:56,830
Otherwise, ShowMe keeps the box.
Refinement never changes the click

11
00:00:56,880 --> 00:01:00,790
point.

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,614
I review the image again, consent to
sharing, and create an expiring link.

13
00:01:13,664 --> 00:01:19,278
On the iPhone, the recipient gets one
instruction and a highlight. They use

14
00:01:19,328 --> 00:01:24,942
it to find the matching control on the
original example. This is a picture,

15
00:01:24,992 --> 00:01:30,606
not remote control, and no
subscription is changed. Another step

16
00:01:30,656 --> 00:01:36,270
needs another screenshot. The link
expires after seven days.

17
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,150
Here is the engineering I built during
the hackathon. The browser re-encodes

18
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:56,350
the reviewed image, and the API client
requires that redaction result. Tests

19
00:01:56,400 --> 00:02:00,550
inspect the uploaded payload. The SAM
agreement gate rejects inconsistent

20
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:04,750
refinement.

21
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,654
On prompt version three, the click
point landed inside the labelled

22
00:02:16,704 --> 00:02:21,358
target in 89.9 percent of 1,172
held-out grounding examples. When the

23
00:02:21,408 --> 00:02:26,062
target was absent, it said “not
visible” in 151 of 200 probes, 75.5

24
00:02:26,112 --> 00:02:30,766
percent. Most of the rest still got a
highlight, and we publish those

25
00:02:30,816 --> 00:02:35,470
failures.

26
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,750
ShowMe is for the person who receives
the screenshot: review locally, send

27
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:51,550
one clear step, and let the recipient
stay in control. Try the live app and

28
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:56,350
inspect the code.
